the first thing I want to ask you about  his permits what are they  no we the only thing we had permits for  were to shoot on the elevator train and  the way we got that was we went to see  the head of the Transit Authority in New  York and they was not the head he was  the head of public relations and we went  to him my producer and I and we told him  what we wanted to do and we asked  permission to do it and he said you guys  are crazy he said I could never let you  do anything like what you just described  he's a first of all there's never been  an elevated train that was hijacked  there's never been a train crash on the  elevated system in New York and he said  it just would be really difficult and my  producer is Sicilian and he said how  difficult and the guy said $40,000 and a  one-way ticket to Jamaica and I asked  him why a one-way ticket and he said  because if I let you do what you just  told me on that train I will be fired  and I want to live the rest of my life  in Jamaica and so he did happily ever  after I'm told but we obviously didn't  have $40,000 in the budget that was the  budget of the film was a million and a  half dollars and that I went $300,000  over partly because of bribes like that  which which is why in my introduction I  said it just can't be done legally you  can they'll never be able to make this  movie again some of the things that you  and I talked about I had the pleasure of  interviewing mr. freaking with Sonny  Grosso who was the police officer who  was the inspiration for Roy Scheider's  character in the movie and they they  were explaining to me that everything  that you did was essentially with the  blessing of sonny and eddie egan who's  featured in the film eddie egan is the  real inspiration for Popeye Doyle who  plays gene Hackman's boss in the film  and that essentially as I understand it  Egon and Grosso were following you  around town and making sure that you  didn't get arrested for everything you  were doing while you were doing it  we had a egan and grosso who we called  Doyle and Russo because of some very  timid lawyers at 20th Century Fox we had  their rights but we still had to change  their names and change the names of the  bad guys but I think what Sonny told you  is is probably true the case took place  ten years before I made the film and the  case took part over a ten-month period  and the movie runs a hundred minutes and  I think you asked Sonny how accurate was  the film and he said about 95 percent  accurate and what he meant by that was  the film is an impression of what  happened in those ten months an  impression done in a hundred minutes the  only things that did not happen in the  case were the chase scene I felt we  needed that or we would have nothing but  a police surveillance picture and and  police surveillance is like watching  paint dry it is so boring or I must  point out you have about 70 minutes of  that in your movie surveillance it  doesn't seem to ever get boring guys  just well but and so  we knew we needed to have a chase but I  wasn't sure until a couple of weeks  before we started shooting what that  chase was gonna be and one day my  producer and I took a walk from 86th  Street on the east side of New York and  we walked for 55 blocks south and we  said we're not gonna stop we're not  gonna turn back until we can think up a  chase scene and we heard the subway  rumbling beneath our feet we saw the  smoke rising from the streets all this  traffic crowds of people that make up  New York  everywhere and we started to improvise  the chase and that's what we shot you  you and I spoke about the genesis of the  car chase and it's the bits the  signature sequence of the of the movie  obviously the the stuntman who was who  was doing a lot of driving the sequences  is the guy who plays the FBI agent in  the film yeah his name again  bill Hickman bill Hickman and bill  Hickman if you're really obsessive like  I am you'll notice that bill Hickman is  also the driver in Bullitt and he's also  the driver in the 7-ups with Roy  Scheider if you've never seen it's  another great car chase from your  producer  that you had talked to me a little bit  about the conversation that led to some  of the key shots in that sequence well I  didn't storyboard this chase I didn't  write it down it wasn't in any script  but we went to various locations I was a  guy named fat Thomas who gets credit on  the screen at the end as location  manager fat Thomas was a 425 pound  bookmaker  in New York who had been arrested 52  times for bookmaking with one conviction  and he but he knew New York like the  back of his hand  and he took me around and showed me all  these  areas around the area that I got  permission to film the chase  it was the Stillwell Avenue line which  at Bay 50th Street which is just a few  blocks from Coney Island in Brooklyn and  fat Thomas took me to that area and  showed me all around and I chose the  locations most of them under the L  tracks that were there and then other  ones that I had also seen that weren't  in that area that I just sort of threw  in and I would take the crew to all of  these locations about a week before we  started the picture and then we talked  about what could happen and we made some  notes and then we just went out and did  it and there were a lot of accidents  that occurred we had no permits to shoot  the chase none we had no permits from  the city to be on the streets at all but  I had these off-duty cops with me and so  if anything went sour they would just  show a badge and the problems would go  away but we had no permission to do  anything that you see but anyway when we  finished shooting everything that I had  planned I was looking at the rushes and  I thought that it was not very good I  thought it was pretty lame stuff and one  day when we were supposedly finished  bill Hickman and I went to a bar  downtown and had a drink and he said to  me well boss you know he was about 15  years older than me but he called me  boss he said what do you think of the  chase we shot and I said bill I don't  think we've got it I think it's not very  good and you know it's not your fault  it's basically not as exciting as I as I  would have hold and he said all right he  said put the car out there under the El  tracks tomorrow morning at 8 o'clock and  you get in the car with me and I'll show  you some driving so we were gonna shoot  somewhere else that day but I went to  the production manager and I said let's  put the car out on this under the  Stillwell Avenue tracks and we mounted  one camera on the bumper of the car I  operated the camera over bill Hickman  shoulder because both Owen royzman and  Ricky Bravo were married with children  and I was not at the time and we had  another camera mounted in the passenger  seat and that was it and bill Hickman  then drove 26 blocks through city  traffic at 90 miles an hour now we had  there was no warrant there was a kind it  was a warning to pedestrians we had a  police gumball on the top of the car a  siren and so we did have that but we  blew through 26 blocks of traffic  without any clearances no extras and no  permission the only thing that was  staged was the woman with the baby  carriage which was the easiest I would  hope so I think that baby may have grown  up by now no there was no baby you know  that yeah this is the Motion Picture  Academy you know there was no baby but  you thought there was a baby

